1
00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:04.337
In this lesson, you will learn about what
DSPy is,

2
00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:08.341
and more importantly,
what is special about DSPy and why

3
00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:12.078
many developers are using DSPy
to power their GenAI apps.

4
00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:15.115
Let's dive. So what is DSPy?

5
00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:18.651
At a very high level,
DSPy is a GenAI authoring framework

6
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.654
that simplifies
the development of GenAI applications.

7
00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:25.125
You can use it
to build a RAG system to agent.

8
00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:28.495
But as a user, I would ask this
very natural question?

9
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.930
Are we reinventing the wheel?

10
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.600
We already have
so many options over there.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.503
The answer is no. Let's explore together.

12
00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:39.305
Before we learn
about the specialty of the DSPy,

13
00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:43.476
let's talk about the problems
we're facing. Since late 2022,

14
00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.845
due to the success of genAI models,

15
00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.715
there came the rise of compound AI system.

16
00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:53.353
Here you can see a question-answering
system and also a code generator.

17
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.924
Compound AI system is a system
that has multiple modules.

18
00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:02.095
Each module handles a subtask,
which can make LLM calls,

19
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.697
or could just be a regular tool calling.

20
00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:09.269
Combining all these modules together,
we form a big and powerful AI system

21
00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.471
that can handle complex
tasks like retrieval augmented

22
00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:12.639
that can handle complex
tasks like retrieval augmented

23
00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:15.975
generation or code generator
as you see in the picture.

24
00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:20.046
When building a compound AI system,
we're all facing the problem

25
00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:21.881
from prompt engineering.

26
00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:25.952
We do prompt engineering
because we know that with a better prompt,

27
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.955
we'll get a better result
from the language model.

28
00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:33.593
But prompt engineering could be very messy
because we are tweaking the strings

29
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.896
and we don't really know what changes
make an actual difference.

30
00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:41.568
In practice, that usually means
we end up iterating over 50

31
00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:45.972
or 100 or even more prompts,
and each prompt could be very long,

32
00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:50.243
like tens of thousands of words.
Making that even worse,

33
00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.246
if we switch a model,
then we need to start over

34
00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.417
because prompt engineering is heavily
biased towards a language model.

35
00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:02.155
As a summary prompt engineering
is both brittle and time-consuming.

36
00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:05.725
The other problem comes from the framework
itself.

37
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:10.396
Frameworks are very powerful
because I simplify and standardize

38
00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:14.200
the experience of building things
like agents or RAG and so on.

39
00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:15.969
But there have been more

40
00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.972
and more complaints
that I don't like the use of frameworks,

41
00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:22.208
because instead of value, I'm
seeing more trouble.

42
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.445
One very solid concern
is that I'm being forced

43
00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:29.449
to learn the framework contract,
which sometimes means unnecessary

44
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:32.886
overhead for me, rather than keeping me
focused on the logics.

45
00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:37.423
And it's really hard to migrate
off the framework existing code

46
00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:40.093
if I decide to switch over to another one.

47
00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:43.096
To address both problems
then came the DSPy.

48
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.032
Now let's answer the question what is DSPy

49
00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.168
again with more details.

50
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:53.168
DSPy is a flexible, lightweight framework
that simplifies

51
00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:57.944
the interaction with LM and provides
automatic program optimization,

52
00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:01.781
including prompt optimization and LM
weights finetuning

53
00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:06.686
through the DSPy optimizer. DSPy
also provides seamless productization

54
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.758
support like streaming async,
and so on. We provide you

55
00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:14.761
with the building blocks
for AI applications in DSPy,

56
00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.931
but we don't restrict
that the way you build the applications.

57
00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:22.001
It's both easy to migrate to DSPy
and migrate off DSPy.

58
00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:25.104
We have three special things inside DSPy:

59
00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.840
And the first one is LM-agnostic

60
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.111
programing
instead of LM-biased prompting. Basically

61
00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:36.015
instead of doing
prompt engineering, DSPy interacts with our LLM

62
00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:40.987
by defining input fields
and the output fields. In DSPy context,

63
00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:44.090
you can choose how I'm and the point has a
well engineered

64
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.527
RESTful API.
But different from traditional APIs,

65
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.863
the input and output are defined
at the client side.

66
00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:51.631
Don't worry

67
00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.634
if this is not fully clear yet,
we'll cover this

68
00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:55.868
in the next lesson.

69
00:03:55.868 --> 00:04:00.406
The second part is seamless
productization through both native DSPy features

70
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:04.177
like streaming, cache
and also through the close integration

71
00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:05.578
with MLflow.

72
00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.481
MLflow is an ML and AI ops tool

73
00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:11.818
that streamlines end-to-end development
of AI applications.

74
00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:17.118
For example, helps debugging AI programs
by MLflow tracing, tracking development

75
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.928
MLflow experiments, and deploy
the application with MLflow deployments.

76
00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.530
We'll learn more about it in lesson three.

77
00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:27.200
And in lesson four, you'll

78
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.869
learn about automatic program
optimization

79
00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:32.772
help to build your applications with DSPy.

80
00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.575
You can create a DSPy optimizer
and then apply it

81
00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:38.845
on your program and automatically
get quality improvements.

82
00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:41.481
DSPy is trusted by industry.

83
00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:45.051
We have many successful integration
with lots of enterprise users.

84
00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:50.156
Please check it out more at
DSPy.ai/community/use-cases.

85
00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.158
That's all about this lesson. In

86
00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:56.129
the next lesson you'll learn
how to build your GenAI applications in DSPy.

87
00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:56.763
See you there.

